{"decision":"PROPOSE","invalid_operation":null,"observations":["All three source questions ask for the number of distinct values obtainable by fully parenthesizing a fixed sequence of operands and binary operators (+,-,*) without reordering.","The shared interface supplies operands (integers) and operators (len(operands)-1 symbols from +,-,*), which exactly parameterizes the expression.","The requested quantity is the cardinality of the set of results over all full binary parenthesizations of the operator sequence.","This is a standard interval dynamic-programming problem: for each contiguous subexpression, compute the set of achievable values by splitting at each operator and combining left/right value sets.","Because operands are bounded (|value|<=10, length<=8) and only +,-,* are used, value sets stay small enough for exact set-based DP; no floating point is needed.","Duplicates must be collapsed per interval (sets), since the question counts distinct values only once."],"procedure_plan":"Implement solve(parameters): read operands a[0..n-1] and operators op[0..n-2]. If n==1 return 1 (single value). Build dp[i][j] = set of distinct integer values from fully parenthesizing a[i..j]. Initialize dp[i][i]={a[i]}. For length L from 2 to n, for each i, j=i+L-1, for each split k in [i, j-1]: combine every x in dp[i][k] with every y in dp[k+1][j] using op[k] (+,-,*), inserting results into dp[i][j]. Return the size of dp[0][n-1]. Use Python ints (arbitrary precision) to avoid overflow. Complexity O(n^3 * S^2) where S is max set size; fine for n<=8.","replacement_operation":null,"uncertainty":"Set sizes could in principle grow, but with n<=8 and small operands the exact DP is tractable; worst-case set size is bounded by the number of parenthesizations (Catalan(7)=429) per interval, so combination work is modest. No ambiguity in the mathematical condition: full parenthesization preserving order, distinct values counted once."}